// The Memory lesson used a pointer without explaining it: `malloc` returned an
// address, `as ulong*` said what lived there, and `values[i]` reached it.
// This is that piece on its own.
//
// A pointer is an address. `&value` produces the address of something, and
// indexing a pointer reaches what is at it: `p[0]` is the value itself (the
// same as `*p`), which is why an allocated block indexes like an array.
//
// In Jux all of that is `unsafe`-only (Layout-ABI §L.6). A pointer VALUE may
// be stored, passed and compared with `null` anywhere; taking an address,
// reading through one or writing through one needs an `unsafe` context.

// A `long*` may be written through, so this changes the caller's variable
// rather than a copy of it. Marking the whole function `unsafe` makes its
// body an unsafe context, and makes calling it an unsafe operation too.
//
// The modifier is written after `public`: a declaration that STARTS with
// `unsafe` is read as an `unsafe { }` statement today (B45).
public unsafe void bump(long* target) {
    target[0] += 1;
}

// The checked narrowing the Convert lesson promised. `as` is silent when a
// value does not fit; this writes the converted value through a pointer and
// returns whether anything was lost, which it finds out by widening the
// result back and comparing. Note carefully which way round the answer reads,
// as in Rux: the flag is true when the conversion FAILED.
public unsafe bool convertChecked(long value, byte* target) {
    *target = value as byte;
    return (*target as long) != value;
}

void main() {
    long value = 41;

    unsafe {
        // Rux has two pointer types, `*int` (read only) and `*var int` (may
        // write), split the way `let` and `var` are. Jux has one, `T*`, which
        // may always write: a pointer is the tool for talking to C, and C's
        // `const` is a promise the callee makes, not something the pointer
        // enforces. Reading and writing look the same.
        long* reader = &value;
        print($"read through long*   ${reader[0]}");

        long* writer = &value;
        writer[0] = 42;
    }
    print($"after writing        ${value}");

    // Because the address was passed rather than the value, the function
    // changed `value` itself. Nothing was returned.
    unsafe {
        bump(&value);
    }
    print($"after bump           ${value}");

    // That is what an out-parameter is. Jux also has a safe spelling for it,
    // `out byte target` (the `out` keyword, §M.4), which needs no `unsafe`
    // and is checked to be written on every path; the pointer form is shown
    // here because it is what the safe one is built on.
    byte narrowed = 0;
    bool lost100 = false;
    bool lost300 = false;
    unsafe {
        lost100 = convertChecked(100, &narrowed);
    }
    print($"100 into byte  lost ${lost100}  value ${narrowed}");

    unsafe {
        lost300 = convertChecked(300, &narrowed);
    }
    print($"300 into byte  lost ${lost300}  value ${narrowed}");

    // 300 does not fit, so the flag is true and `narrowed` holds the wrapped
    // 44, the same 44 that `300 as byte` produces silently. The difference is
    // that this time the program was told.
    if (lost300) {
        print("so the program can react instead of carrying on with 44");
    }

    // A pointer can also point at nothing, which is what `null` means and what
    // the Memory lesson checked for after `malloc`. Comparing with `null` is
    // safe; reaching through a null pointer is not caught by the compiler.
    long* nothing = null;
    print($"is it null?          ${nothing == null}");
}
